Recent research demonstrates successful GPS spoofing with commodity
hardware, achieving error rates \textless{} 1m.

\textbf{1.3 Technical Contributions}

I introduce several novel technical contributions:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} A zero-knowledge range proof protocol specifically
optimized for location verification, with proof size O(log n) and
verification time O(log n), where n is the precision parameter.

2.\hspace{0pt} A decentralized witness network with Byzantine fault
tolerance up to f \textless{} n/2 malicious nodes, improving upon the
theoretical maximum of existing systems.

3.\hspace{0pt} A novel cryptographic commitment scheme that enables
location proofs with perfect completeness and computational soundness
under standard cryptographic assumptions.

4.\hspace{0pt} Formal security proofs demonstrating that the protocol
achieves non-repudiation under the discrete logarithm assumption.

Let me define the key primitives formally:

\textbf{Definition 1 (Location Proof)}\\
A location proof is a tuple (c, π) where:\\
●\hspace{0pt} c is a commitment to location l using randomness r: c =
Commit(l, r) ●\hspace{0pt} π is a zero-knowledge proof that l lies
within a valid range {[}a, b{]}\hspace{0pt} such that Verify(c, π, {[}a,
b{]}) = 1 iff l ∈ {[}a, b{]}

\textbf{Definition 2 (Witness Network)}\\
A witness network W is a set of n nodes \{w₁, ..., w\} where:\\
●\hspace{0pt} Each w\_i maintains a key pair (pk\_i, sk\_i)\\
●\hspace{0pt} The network achieves Byzantine agreement with probability
≥ 1 - 2\^{}(-λ) ●\hspace{0pt} No subset of size ≤ n/2 can forge valid
proofs

The rest of this paper is organized as follows: In \textbf{Section 2}, I
review related work and position OLP within the theoretical foundations
of location verification systems. \textbf{Section 3}

