\begin{quote}
4. Security Analysis

\textbf{4.1 Security Model and Assumptions}

Let me formally define the security model under which OLP operates:

\emph{Definition 4.1 (Security Model):} The protocol assumes:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} A PPT adversary A with control over f \textless{} n/2
witness nodes\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Secure channels between honest participants\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Synchronous network with maximum delay Δ\\
4.\hspace{0pt} Standard cryptographic primitives (DDH assumption,
collision-resistant hash functions)

\textbf{4.2 Attack Models}

I analyze the protocol\textquotesingle s security against several attack
vectors:

4.2.1 Location Spoofing Attacks

\emph{Theorem 4.1 (Spoofing Resistance):} Under the DDH assumption, no
PPT adversary can forge a valid location proof with probability
\textgreater{} negl(λ).

\emph{Proof:} By contradiction. Assume adversary A succeeds with
non-negligible probability ε.

Construct reduction B that breaks DDH:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Given DDH instance (g,ga,gb,g\^{}c)\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Embed challenge in commitment: c = g\^{}l · (ga)r\\
3.\hspace{0pt} If A forges valid proof, extract discrete log\\
4.\hspace{0pt} Contradiction to DDH assumption

4.2.2 Witness Collusion Attacks

\emph{Definition 4.2 (k-Collusion):} A set of k witnesses collude if
they coordinate to generate false proofs.

\emph{Theorem 4.2 (Collusion Resistance):} The protocol is secure
against k-collusion for k \textless{} n/2 witnesses.

\textbf{DOI}: \textbf{10.13140/RG.2.2.30697.20324/1} 19
\end{quote}

\begin{longtable}[]{@{}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 8\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2000}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 8\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2000}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 8\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2000}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 8\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2000}}
  >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{(\columnwidth - 8\tabcolsep) * \real{0.2000}}@{}}
