\end{minipage} \\
\midrule()
\endhead
\bottomrule()
\end{longtable}

\begin{quote}
\emph{Proof:}\\
Let p\_honest be the probability of selecting an honest witness.

For k witnesses:\\
P(all corrupt) = (1-p\_honest)ᵏ ≤ (1/2)ᵏ ≤ negl(λ)

4.2.3 Replay Attacks
\end{quote}

\emph{Lemma 4.1:} The protocol prevents replay attacks through unique
nonces and timestamps.

\begin{quote}
\emph{Proof:} Each PPR includes:\\
●\hspace{0pt} Unique nonce r ← \{0,1\}λ\\
●\hspace{0pt} Timestamp t\\
●\hspace{0pt} Hash h = H(r\textbar\textbar t\textbar\textbar location)

The probability of collision is:\\
P(collision) ≤ q²/2λ where q is total number of queries.

\textbf{4.3 Formal Security Properties}

I now prove the core security properties of OLP:

4.3.1 Non-Repudiation

\emph{Theorem 4.3 (Non-Repudiation):} Given a valid proof p, the
probability of successful repudiation is negligible:\\
Pr{[}Repudiate(p) = 1{]} ≤ negl(λ)

\emph{Proof:} By sequence of games:\\
Game 0: Original non-repudiation game\\
Game 1: Replace commitment with random value\\
Game 2: Replace ZKRP with simulation

\textbar Pr{[}G0{]} - Pr{[}G1{]}\textbar{} ≤ Advᴰᴰᴴ(λ)\\
\textbar Pr{[}G1{]} - Pr{[}G2{]}\textbar{} ≤ Advᶻᴷᴾ(λ)

Therefore, Pr{[}G0{]} ≤ negl(λ)
\end{quote}

