\begin{quote}
4.3.2 Privacy

\emph{Theorem 4.4 (Location Privacy):} The protocol achieves
computational location privacy: For any locations l₁,l₂, the
distributions of their proofs are computationally\\
indistinguishable.

\emph{Proof:} Through a hybrid argument:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} H₀: Real proof for l₁\\
2.\hspace{0pt} H₁: Replace commitment with random value\\
3.\hspace{0pt} H₂: Replace ZKRP with simulation\\
4.\hspace{0pt} H₃: Real proof for l₂

Each transition is indistinguishable under DDH and ZKRP zero-knowledge.

4.3.3 Soundness

\emph{Theorem 4.5 (Soundness):} If at least t witnesses are honest, the
protocol achieves computational soundness:\\
Pr{[}Verify(Forge(l)) = 1{]} ≤ negl(λ)

\emph{Proof:} Reduction to discrete logarithm problem:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Given g, g\^{}x\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Embed challenge in witness responses\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Extract discrete log from successful forge\\
4.\hspace{0pt} Contradiction

\textbf{DOI}: \textbf{10.13140/RG.2.2.30697.20324/1} 21
\end{quote}

