\begin{quote}
6. Scalability Analysis

\textbf{6.1 Computational Complexity}

Let me analyze the computational complexity of key protocol components:

\emph{Theorem 6.1 (Complexity Bounds):} The protocol achieves:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Proof Generation: O(log n) for range size n\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Verification: O(k log n) for k witnesses\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Witness Selection: O(log N) for N total witnesses

\emph{Proof:} Through amortized analysis:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Range proof generation uses Bulletproofs: O(log n)
2.\hspace{0pt} Verification requires k independent checks: O(k log n)
3.\hspace{0pt} Witness selection uses binary tree: O(log N)

\textbf{6.2 Communication Complexity}

\emph{Theorem 6.2 (Communication Efficiency):} The total communication
complexity is: C(n,k) = O(k·log n) bits

Where:\\
●\hspace{0pt} n is the range size\\
●\hspace{0pt} k is the number of witnesses

\emph{Proof:} Break down by components:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} PPR size: O(log n) for range proof\\
2.\hspace{0pt} k witness responses: O(k)\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Aggregation overhead: O(log k)\hspace{0pt}\\
Total: O(k·log n)

\textbf{6.3 Network Scalability}

I introduce a novel sharding mechanism for network scalability:
\end{quote}

