\begin{quote}
\emph{Theorem 6.3 (Sharding Security):} The sharding mechanism maintains
security with probability ≥ 1-2\^{}(-λ) if:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Each shard has ≥ k witnesses\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Inter-shard communication is bounded by O(log m)\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Shard size grows with O(√N)

\emph{Proof:} Through probabilistic analysis:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Model witness distribution as Poisson process\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Apply Chernoff bounds for concentration\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Show security reduction to single-shard case

\textbf{6.4 Performance Bounds}

\emph{Theorem 6.4 (Performance Guarantees):} Under typical network
conditions (Δ delay, B bandwidth), the protocol guarantees:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Proof Generation Time: T\_gen ≤ c₁log(n) + c₂k\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Verification Time: T\_ver ≤ c₃k·log(n)\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Network Latency: L ≤ 2Δ + c₄(k/B)

Where c₁,c₂,c₃,c₄ are system constants.

\emph{Proof:} Through queueing theory analysis:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Model system as M/M/k queue\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Apply Little\textquotesingle s Law for latency bounds\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Consider worst-case network conditions
\end{quote}

