\begin{quote}
7. Future Research Directions

\textbf{7.1 Post-Quantum Security}

The current protocol relies on classical cryptographic assumptions. I
identify key challenges for post-quantum security:

\emph{Definition 7.1 (Post-Quantum Security):} A protocol is
quantum-secure if no quantum adversary running in time poly(λ) can break
its security with probability \textgreater{} negl(λ).

\emph{Open Problem 7.1:} Construct efficient post-quantum ZKRPs for
location verification with: ●\hspace{0pt} Proof size: O(log n)\\
●\hspace{0pt} Verification time: O(log n)\\
●\hspace{0pt} Quantum security under LWE assumption

Potential approaches include:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} Lattice-based range proofs\\
2.\hspace{0pt} STARK-based constructions\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Quantum-resistant commitment schemes

\textbf{7.2 Dynamic Witness Networks}

\emph{Definition 7.2 (Dynamic Security):} A protocol maintains security
under churn rate ρ if: Pr{[}Break(t+Δt) \textbar{} Secure(t){]} ≤
negl(λ) for churn ρ·Δt

\emph{Open Problem 7.2:} Design efficient witness rotation mechanisms
that: 1.\hspace{0pt} Maintain security under ρ churn\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Require O(log N) communication\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Preserve k-anonymity

\textbf{7.3 Formal Verification}

I identify key properties requiring formal verification:

\emph{Definition 7.3 (Verification Goals):} Prove the following
properties: 1.\hspace{0pt} Safety: ∀ valid proofs p, Verify(p) = 1\\
2.\hspace{0pt} Liveness: Valid proofs eventually verify\\
3.\hspace{0pt} Non-interference: Proofs don\textquotesingle t leak
information
\end{quote}

