\begin{quote}
8. Conclusions and Impact

\textbf{8.1 Theoretical Contributions}

This paper makes several novel contributions to location verification
theory:

\emph{1.\hspace{0pt} Zero-Knowledge Constructions:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} First optimal-size range proofs for location data
○\hspace{0pt} Novel commitment scheme with non-repudiation ○\hspace{0pt}
Efficient batch verification techniques

\emph{2.\hspace{0pt} Security Bounds:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} Tight bounds on witness collusion resistance\\
○\hspace{0pt} Optimal communication complexity\\
○\hspace{0pt} Information-theoretic privacy guarantees

\emph{3.\hspace{0pt} Formal Framework:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} Rigorous security definitions\\
○\hspace{0pt} Composable protocol design\\
○\hspace{0pt} Formal verification targets

\textbf{8.2 Practical Implications}

The OLP protocol enables several key applications:\\
1.\hspace{0pt} \emph{Decentralized Location Verification:}\hspace{0pt}\\
\hspace{0pt}\\
VerifyLocation(proof) → \{0,1\} with:\\
○\hspace{0pt} No trusted parties\\
○\hspace{0pt} Privacy preservation\\
○\hspace{0pt} Non-repudiation\hspace{0pt}

\emph{2.\hspace{0pt} Privacy-Preserving Presence:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} k-anonymous presence proofs\\
○\hspace{0pt} Selective disclosure mechanisms\\
○\hspace{0pt} Revocable anonymity

\emph{3.\hspace{0pt} Scalable Infrastructure:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} Sharded witness networks\\
○\hspace{0pt} Dynamic participant sets
\end{quote}

