A.4 Proof of Theorem 2.3 (Blockchain Limitation)

\emph{Theorem 2.3 (Blockchain Limitation):} Any blockchain-based
location verification system must either:\\
a) Reveal location history on-chain, or\\
b) Rely on trusted oracles

\emph{Proof:} By contradiction. Assume there exists a blockchain-based
location verification system S that achieves both privacy (no location
history revealed) and trustlessness (no trusted oracles).
Let\textquotesingle s construct a proof through a series of steps:

\textbf{1.\hspace{0pt} Setup:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} Let L = \{l₁, ..., lₙ\} be a sequence of location claims\\
○\hspace{0pt} Let B be the blockchain state\\
○\hspace{0pt} Let V be the set of verifiers\\
2.\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Information Flow Analysis:}\hspace{0pt}\\
For any verifier v ∈ V to validate location l:\\
○\hspace{0pt} Either location data must be directly available on-chain\\
○\hspace{0pt} Or some external entity must attest to its validity\\
\textbf{3.\hspace{0pt} Privacy Requirement:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} By assumption, L is not revealed on-chain\\
○\hspace{0pt} Therefore, ∀ blocks b ∈ B: Entropy(L\textbar b) =
Entropy(L)\\
○\hspace{0pt} i.e., the blockchain contains no information about L\\
\textbf{4.\hspace{0pt} Verification Requirement:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} For correct verification: Pr{[}Verify(l) = 1 \textbar{} l
valid{]} = 1\\
○\hspace{0pt} By blockchain consensus: ∀v,v\textquotesingle{} ∈ V:
Verify\_v(l) = Verify\_v\textquotesingle(l) \textbf{5.\hspace{0pt}
Contradiction:}\\
○\hspace{0pt} If L is not on-chain (by privacy), verifiers must obtain
location data externally\\
○\hspace{0pt} Let O be the set of external data providers\\
○\hspace{0pt} For consensus: ∀v ∈ V: v must trust O\\
○\hspace{0pt} Therefore, O is a set of trusted oracles\\
6.\hspace{0pt} \textbf{Formal Contradiction:}\hspace{0pt}
\end{quote}

\textbf{DOI}: \textbf{10.13140/RG.2.2.30697.20324/1} 34

